I shipped today's entry, **Loom for lonely retirees**, and it passes validation (I re-ran the check after my last edit).

- **Roll:** Loom × lonely retirees, slug `loom-for-lonely-retirees`. The first draw was a new pair, so no rerolls.
- **Concept:** Natter, a video-message app for retirees that promises every message gets watched. When the family doesn't watch, Craig does; he's a salaried staff viewer. The three features are guaranteed viewership, "forehead-first framing" and automatic chapters ("Is this on").
- **Design:** a VHS rental-tape look with the blue on-screen menu of an old VCR, in light mode. Palette is VCR blue `#2340C8`, record-button red `#D7372A` and a beige background `#EFE6D4`. Fonts are Bowlby One, Karla and VT323. None of it repeats anything from the last 7 entries.
- **Images:** used all 4, and each passed on its first attempt:
  - the hero, a retiree leaning far too close to his webcam, shown inside a CSS TV set;
  - two video thumbnails for the demo;
  - one testimonial headshot.
- **Demo:** a library of the user's sent videos. You can filter by recipient, search and sort, and each video shows who watched it and for how long. Clicking one opens a player with a seek bar, chapters you can jump to, and a chart showing who watched how much of it. The "Nudge" button gets firmer each time you press it. The recorder has a "lean in" slider that switches to Portrait Mode once your forehead covers 70% of the frame.
- **Gallery:** rebuilt with 111 slides, and the header count now reads 111. `state/history.json` got one new entry with no other changes, and the run summary was added to the end of `state/runs/2026-10-02.log`.
- **Problem I fixed:** the first validation failed on `demo.html`. The validator read a piece of JavaScript that builds an image path as a reference to an image file that doesn't exist. I changed the demo to set those image paths when the page runs, and it passed after that.
- **Visual check:** I took the two allowed screenshots and made one fix: an empty pop-up message was showing as a small dark square on the demo, and it's now hidden until it has text.

I didn't run any git commands; the wrapper handles that.

